{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788273648,"nonce":"zYNnkXRTMAIjezNTi2Dv80A2hQ8=","message":"THE PRE-AUTHORIZED CHOICE, MADE: the whitelist was attempted, measured, and\nREJECTED. The narrow fix ships, sharpened. Branch `spec/site-routine`, commit\n`280a0cb`. Session 15, worker. Builds clean (`dotnet build -c Release`, 0/0).\n\nComment #1 said \"do not come back for permission either way; the fallback is not\na failure, it is the measurement this issue asked for.\" Here is the measurement.\n\n\nComment #1 named the fallback trigger: \"a verb that forwards args to a sub-verb\n... is the whitelist being too invasive, and **one such case is enough** to take\nthe fallback for the whole change rather than special-casing it.\" The audit found\na structural one, not a case.\n\nMeasured statically over the shipped tree and every shipped suite — statically\nbecause a worker launches nothing, which is also why the DYNAMIC finding below\nmatters:\n\n- **120 registered verbs** (the issue said 119; one has landed since).\n- **22 handlers read no argument at their own call sites.** Some take none\n  (`pause`, `version`, `status`, `trade-cancel`); the rest forward `ctx.Args`\n  wholesale into a helper — `advance` into `TimeDriver.Start`, `bill-set` and\n  `bill-remove` into `BillVerbs`' helpers, `draft` / `undraft` /\n  `clear-priority-work` into `PawnOrderVerbs`', `comms-choose`, `dialog-choose`,\n  `letter-*`, `trade`, `storage-unlink`, `policies`, `catalog-dump`, `areas`.\n- **88 (verb, key) pairs that the shipped suites actually send, across 53 verbs,\n  are read through shared helpers rather than at the handler.** `pawns`, `pawn`,\n  `thing`, `target`, `bench`, `ticks`, `max_tps`, `letter`, `quest`, `kind`,\n  `rect`, `targets`, `index`, `uid`, `negotiator`, `hours` … every one\n  legitimate, none of them derivable from the handler body.\n- **Five suite call sites build their argument dict at RUNTIME** — `advance`\n  (`accept/s13-mod-surface.py`), `designate` (`8b0b88f-already-designated.py`),\n  `quest-accept` (`3.5-dialog-verbs.py`), `surgery-add` (`3.6-bills-storage.py`),\n  `things` (`70ac258-things-stable-order.py`). A whitelist cannot be validated\n  statically against those at all, and a worker cannot validate it dynamically.\n\nSo a declared arg set could be neither derived from the code nor checked against\nit. It would be a second source of truth over 120 verbs — and this repo has a\nworked example of what that costs: the `Build:` tally in the workspace CLAUDE.md\nwent stale three times in one day and misled two review agents. Worse, its drift\nmode is asymmetric in the wrong direction: **a missing declaration REFUSES a\nlegitimate call**, mid-run, on a live bench. Shipping that unmeasured against a\nten-day run is not the trade this project makes.\n\nTo be precise about what is and is not claimed: this is not \"a whitelist is\nimpossible\". It is \"a whitelist here needs 120 hand-written declarations that\nnothing can check, whose failure mode is refusing correct calls, and I cannot run\nthe suites that would bound the blast radius.\"\n\n\nThe narrow fix, past what the issue asked for.\n\n- **`pos_source` in the envelope AND the journal row**, for `dev:spawn-thing` and\n  `dev:spawn-pawn`: `\"arg\" | \"anchor-default\" | \"stockpile\"`. Three sources reach\n  `target` in `dev:spawn-thing` and two are not the caller's coordinates. The\n  fallback is now never silent even when it was intended, which is the\n  Acceptance's fallback bullet verbatim.\n- **A NEAR MISS IS REFUSED rather than defaulted past.** `Dev.PosArg` refuses\n  `at`, `cell`, `position`, `loc`, `location` and `where` when the real key is\n  absent, naming the key and suggesting the right one. That is this issue's own\n  worked example — `at` -\u003e `pos` on `dev:spawn-thing`, the call that produced\n  three wrong results in a row on bench `20260901T121508` — satisfied at the\n  entry point of the one argument whose default is dangerous. Audited over all\n  six `PosArg` call sites and over `StarterKit`'s and `world-fixture`'s forwarded\n  dicts, which pass only `pos` and `around`, so none of the six aliases is read\n  by anything that defaults a cell.\n- Exposed as **`VerbArgs.NearMiss(key, aliases)`** so any verb with a defaulted\n  positional adopts it in one line, in the same class whose header already says\n  \"present with the wrong type =\u003e bad-args even for optionals — the caller is a\n  program, and silently coercing its mistakes hides them\". The key gets the same\n  disposition as the value.\n- **Aliases come from the CALL SITE and are never guessed globally.** A global\n  list overlapping a real argument name would refuse a CORRECT call, which is a\n  worse bug than this one — and the proof is live in the same branch:\n  `site-survey` reads BOTH `pos` and `at` legitimately.\n\n\nKept as a default, deliberately. \"A fixture that has to compute its own\ncoordinates is a fixture nobody writes\", and `StarterKit` depends on it. What\nchanges is that it can no longer be reached by a typo and can no longer fire\nsilently. That is the middle the issue's own fallback text asks for; removing it\nwould break staging for no gain now that both guards exist.\n\n\n- ✅ (fallback bullet) `dev:spawn-thing` reports which source its cell came from,\n  and the journal row carries it. Also `dev:spawn-pawn`.\n- ✅ (worked example) `at` -\u003e `pos` on `dev:spawn-thing` is refused with\n  `bad-args` naming `at` and suggesting `pos`.\n- ⚠️ (whitelist bullets) \"ANY unrecognised argument key on ANY verb\" is NOT\n  satisfied and deliberately so — see the measurement.\n- ✅ No existing suite can break: the only behaviour change is on calls that\n  today silently use a default, and the aliases are read by nothing.\n  **This is a static argument, not a run.** The suites were audited call site by\n  call site rather than executed; the orchestrator owns the bench pass.\n\n`accept/s13-mod-surface.py` gains **1.8a-e** (the refusal names `at`, suggests\n`pos`, and publishes no `data` — nothing was mutated) and **1.9a-d**\n(`pos_source` in both the envelope and the row, `arg` vs `anchor-default`).\n`--selftest` 25/25. DESIGN's decisions log carries the numbers.","files":null}]}